        <mxCell id="MQ5YCEGSpXZeGLRsvfkm-29" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=1;jettySize=auto;html=1;exitX=0.5;exitY=1;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=0;entryY=0.5;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="bR7-ODnRsVLOpwfvf6qA-36" target="MQ5YCEGSpXZeGLRsvfkm-14">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="MQ5YCEGSpXZeGLRsvfkm-28" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=1;jettySize=auto;html=1;exitX=0.5;exitY=1;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=0;entryY=0.5;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="MQ5YCEGSpXZeGLRsvfkm-186" target="MQ5YCEGSpXZeGLRsvfkm-14">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
            <mxPoint x="353.7724511446122" y="124.50603842102919" as="sourcePoint" />
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="bR7-ODnRsVLOpwfvf6qA-177" value="&lt;b&gt;PRF Read Logic: &lt;/b&gt;we expect the PRF to have a certain number of read ports. Reads are used to get the source values. Since we want to release source pointers as soon as possible, we can use one fifo per each RS. Once a push is complete, the Issue stage can release the sources and deallocate the entry (dstptr and other bits should be stages in the meanwhile).&amp;nbsp;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;When the request is served, a response will come and the values will be pushed into the RS fifo. While RS fifo entries might be larger than RS entries, they are not CAMs, hence less expensive to mantain. Morever, this way we can streamling the execution and allow for more uops to be dispatched.&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="400" y="2380" width="330" height="200" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="bR7-ODnRsVLOpwfvf6qA-179" value="&lt;b&gt;PRF Write Logic: &lt;/b&gt;differently from reads, write put less pressure on the PRF. Morever, implementing more reading ports is easier than write ports, meaning we will have less write ports (1 probably). If necessary, we could use more FIFOs before the arbiters, decoupling this stage from the execution. When a write is sent to the PRF, we can assume it to be completed in the next clock cycle. Therefore, we can broadcast the dptr at the same time. Remember, that dstptr release is not happening at this point, but only when a dptr is overwritten in the BRM (commit time)." style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="800" y="2055" width="330" height="150" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="bR7-ODnRsVLOpwfvf6qA-180" value="&lt;b&gt;S Update Logic: &lt;/b&gt;here we got the S-map update. Each lane is producing at most 2 srcptrs. They must be decoded and then all put into an or: this will tell us which srcs have been released in this cycle. Since fanout could be very large, we might not want to one hot decode all the lines at once (timing reasons).&amp;nbsp;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;b&gt;Note: &lt;/b&gt;this part could be particularly timing critical.&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-345" y="1516.75" width="330" height="137" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="xmsxfgwGDsNUJGS1wj1p-81" value="" style="group" parent="1" vertex="1" connectable="0">
          <mxGeometry x="-640" y="800" width="102" height="160" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
